# CADDY_TLS_MODE=local: Caddy sits behind a proxy that terminates TLS (Nginx
# Proxy Manager, Traefik, a cloud load balancer), so Let's Encrypt can never
# reach it to validate a certificate. Every site — the domain, its www/
# bookings/dashboard redirect hosts and the meeting host — is issued a
# certificate from Caddy's internal CA instead, and the proxy is configured to
# accept a certificate that is not publicly trusted (Nginx Proxy Manager:
# forward scheme https, port 443, "Ignore Invalid SSL" on). The browser only
# ever sees the proxy's own certificate.
local_certs

# The proxy in front is the only peer Caddy sees, so without this every
# visitor would reach the app as the proxy's address: the per-IP rate limits on
# login, register, forgot-password and magic links would share ONE bucket for
# the whole site (ten bad logins from anyone would lock out every member) and
# audit-log IPs would all be the proxy. `trusted_proxies` makes Caddy read the
# visitor's address from the proxy's X-Forwarded-For, which `{client_ip}` then
# carries to the app (see `header_up` in deploy/caddy/tacbookings-active.caddy).
# CADDY_TRUSTED_PROXIES (docker-compose.yml) lists the proxy's addresses or
# CIDRs; the default `private_ranges` covers a proxy on the same LAN. Never set
# it to a range the public can reach: that would let any visitor forge their IP.
servers {
	trusted_proxies static {$CADDY_TRUSTED_PROXIES:private_ranges}
	client_ip_headers X-Forwarded-For
}
